Path-B prompt token ids
========================

prompt   : "The capital of France is"
token ids: 504 3575 282 4649 314   (5 ids, NO BOS)

SOURCE OF IDS: pure-Rail tokenizer (NOT the HF fallback).
  - Encoder: tok2.rail / tok2_lib.rail (byte-level BPE, copied from the
    read-only rail repo at ~/projects/rail/tools/railml/tok2.rail
    into tools/railml/pathb/).
  - Driver:  tools/railml/pathb/tok_prompt_check.rail
  - Tokenizer file: tools/railml/pathb/smollm2_tokenizer.json
    (the real SmolLM2-135M tokenizer.json, 2,104,556 bytes, HF cache
     snapshot 93efa2f...; loaded via t2_load2 path blen 60000).

VERIFICATION (Rail ids vs HF ground truth):
  Rail t2_encode(tok, "The capital of France is") == [504 3575 282 4649 314]
  HF transformers 5.5.0 AutoTokenizer.encode               == [504 3575 282 4649 314]
  -> BYTE-IDENTICAL. match = PASS.
  detok(ids) round-trips to the original surface string "The capital of France is".
  HF prepends NO BOS for SmolLM2 base (add_bos_token=False); Rail matches.

So the forward path stays fully pure-Rail end to end: pure-Rail tokenizer
produces the ids, no HF fallback was needed for this prompt.

CAVEAT (preserved from the tokenizer contract):
  tok2 does whitespace-attached pre-tokenization only; it does NOT port the
  full GPT-2 pre-tokenization regex. That is exact for this whitespace-
  delimited prompt, but could diverge on punctuation/contraction inputs.
  For such inputs, feed HF ids and label the tokenization as HF.

Build/run to reproduce:
  cd ~/projects/rail-https
  ./rail_native tools/railml/pathb/tok_prompt_check.rail
  RAIL_ARENA_MB=4096 /tmp/rail_out
  ->  rail ids : [504 3575 282 4649 314 ]   match : PASS
